# HyperMarkDown integration boundary

HyperMarkDown remains authored/storage content; [[hql]] is a separate language
that will compute over typed knowledge. Integration is planned, not implemented.

## Existing implementation inspected

The inspected HyperMarkDown checkout was revision
`74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9` of
[ewiger/hypermarkdown](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown).
Paths below refer to that revision:

- [`tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/parse.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/parse.py):
  `parse(path, text, rel)` returns a `Document` with parse diagnostics.
- [`model.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/model.py):
  `Document` retains text, frontmatter, `CardConfig`, headings, anchors, and
  links. Link records include target, fragment, display, embed status, and span.
- [`frontmatter.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/frontmatter.py):
  the reserved `tags`, `use`, `import`, and `nav` keys are checked; other keys
  remain user-owned. This is a partial schema, not a complete record type.
- [`resolve.py`](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/74487940adb82654207cfec4cfd934d03eeee8f9/tools/hmd/src/hypermarkdown/resolve.py):
  `Workspace` owns documents and contextual resolution. Resolution distinguishes
  resolved, unresolved, ambiguous, and escaping references; precedence includes
  named imports, spine, imported search paths, and autodiscovery.

The repository README identifies the Python tool as canonical and the
TypeScript core as a second implementation sharing a conformance corpus.
There is no reason to create another HMD parser in this Rust bootstrap.

## Proposed integration route (unresolved transport)

A host adapter can call the canonical Python library to parse and resolve a
workspace, then expose a versioned projection to Rust through serialization or
an in-process bridge. A protocol/export is work to design, not an existing API
we assume is available. The adapter would translate each `Document` into a
language-facing `Card`, preserving provenance, document structure, metadata,
namespace context, relation outcomes, and diagnostics.

Reserved frontmatter can supply known field types; user fields need an explicit
schema or a deliberate unknown-value policy. Never infer a complete schema from
one example. Preserve ambiguity and missing references instead of choosing a
card silently. Verify span coordinate conventions with Unicode fixtures before
translating Python positions into HQL UTF-8 byte diagnostics.

Keep HMD import/resolution rules in the adapter; `.hql` module semantics are a
separate design. An eventual adapter should use the HMD conformance corpus plus
projection tests. The present core has no HMD dependency or Card placeholder.
See [architecture](../models/domain/architecture.md) and [[design-direction]].
